    "DIAGNOSIS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "The quickness of the jerks and the absence of voluntary influence help differentiating myoclonus from other movement disorders.",
              "pdf_page": 1019,
              "printed_page": 937,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Congenital myoclonus—in neonates or during the first months of life in otherwise healthy dogs. Excitement, voluntary activity, and tactile/auditory stimuli often increase the",
              "pdf_page": 1019,
              "printed_page": 937,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "myoclonus.",
              "pdf_page": 1019,
              "printed_page": 937,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Canine distemper virus— unvaccinated dog; observed before, during, or after the acute CNS disease; more frequently observed later when the animal is otherwise healthy. Myoclonus involves one limb, more than one limb, the facial or the masticatory",
              "pdf_page": 1019,
              "printed_page": 937,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "muscles.",
              "pdf_page": 1019,
              "printed_page": 937,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Lafora disease (progressive myoclonic epilepsy)—neurodegenerative disease that can cause myoclonic jerks. Differentiated on presence of other seizure types (generalized tonic–clonic seizures) and",
              "pdf_page": 1019,
              "printed_page": 937,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "altered mentation.",
              "pdf_page": 1019,
              "printed_page": 937,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Myoclonic epilepsy— jerks usually generalized, proximal more than distal, and flexor more than extensor; exacerbated by sensory stimulus (light, noise) and voluntary movements. Other types of seizures may be present concomitantly.",
              "pdf_page": 1019,
              "printed_page": 937,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
